hifiberry-webui (0.28.0) stable; urgency=medium

  * Player settings can be free text: a player plugin may declare a "text"
    setting (for example a server address), shown as a text field on the
    player card. It is saved when the field is left rather than on every
    keystroke, and the server checks the value against the plugin's length
    limit and pattern.
  * Players with a sign-in of their own (Jellyfin) can be signed in from
    the player card with Quick Connect: Sign in shows a code to enter in
    the app on a phone or computer, the card follows it until it is
    approved, expired or cancelled, and shows the account and server
    afterwards, with Sign out. A sign-in still active on a server that is
    no longer selected is shown with Sign out as well.
  * A player whose setup is unfinished can no longer be switched on, but
    one that is already running can always be switched off. The card says
    "Setup required — sign in" for a player that needs a sign-in, and
    reads the setup again after its settings are saved and whenever it is
    opened, instead of keeping the state from when the page loaded.

 -- HiFiBerry <support@hifiberry.com>  Thu, 12 Nov 2026 17:00:00 +0000

hifiberry-webui (0.27.2) stable; urgency=medium

  * DSP Programs offers the DSP add-on profiles for every card that hosts
    the add-on. Only cards named "DAC2 ..." were mapped to it, so a Digi2
    Pro (or Amp4 Pro, DAC2 HD, ...) with the add-on was shown every
    profile, the DAC+ DSP one included; a Digi2 Pro with that profile on
    its add-on had no S/PDIF output (hifiberry/hifiberry-os#665). Only the
    DAC+ DSP and the Beocreate 4-Channel Amplifier have their own DSP and
    profiles.
  * DSP Programs marks the profile running on the DSP as installed. It
    compared MD5 checksums, which do not match on newer profiles such as
    DSP add-on v15; it now compares the SHA-1 and falls back to MD5 only for
    profiles that carry nothing else. The deploy dialog's "Already
    installed" check never matched and uses the same comparison now.
  * New display setting "Hide volume on Now Playing" (kalaksi).
  * Fixed selecting settings menu items on a touchscreen (kalaksi).

 -- HiFiBerry <support@hifiberry.com>  Tue, 10 Nov 2026 16:36:24 +0000

hifiberry-webui (0.27.1) stable; urgency=medium

  * Unknown /api/ paths now answer 404 with a JSON body instead of falling
    through to the web interface, which served index.html with 200 OK. A
    client probing for an API path could not tell it was missing: the Home
    Assistant integration took the 200 as success and never tried
    /api/audiocontrol/now-playing, so its player always showed idle. Every
    service claims a longer /api/<service>/ prefix, so only paths no service
    serves are affected.

 -- HiFiBerry <support@hifiberry.com>  Sun, 11 Oct 2026 13:57:52 +0000

hifiberry-webui (0.27.0) stable; urgency=medium

  * A Memory Usage card on the System Information page, showing which features
    are using the machine's RAM and which are worth turning off. It reads the
    new GET /api/v1/memory report and degrades to a message rather than an
    error on a device whose configurator is too old to serve it.
  * The card shows the five biggest users flat, largest first, with a
    disclosure for the rest. Showing every feature made it roughly thirty rows
    on a real device and it dominated the page.
  * Expanded, features are grouped three ways rather than two. "Required" now
    means only what ships a descriptor saying so; a unit no descriptor claims
    is listed as unknown functionality instead. Calling tailscaled or
    systemd-journald "required" asserted something the report cannot support.
  * Freed memory is shown as a range and never mixes RAM with swap. A feature
    holding 1.6 MB of RAM and 27 MB of swap used to read as "frees 28 MB" and
    outranked features holding twenty times more RAM, which is bad advice for
    someone trying to relieve memory pressure.
  * Features that can't be turned off show no reclaimable figure at all: the
    audio engine is nearly all shared libraries, so a number there would be
    both large and meaningless.
  * Building the package no longer deletes the developer's node_modules.
    debian/rules cleaned it to keep 268 MB out of the source tarball, but
    dpkg-source runs clean against the working tree rather than a copy, so
    every build wiped it and the next npm command failed with a bare "command
    not found". It is excluded via debian/source/options instead.

 -- HiFiBerry <support@hifiberry.com>  Mi., 09 Sep. 2026 10:02:05 +0000

